Dictionary is a generic collection - because we specify the type of value and key at compile time
Dictionary<int, string>
Key Type = int
Value Type = string

1. How a Dictionary Works Internally
	•	A Dictionary<TKey, TValue> stores data using a hash table.
	•	When you add a key:
	1.	GetHashCode() of the key is calculated.
	2.	That hash is used to determine a bucket index.
	3.	The key-value pair is stored in that bucket.
Example 
dict[p1] = "First";

Flow
p1.GetHashCode() → hash value
hash % bucketCount → bucket index
store key-value in that bucket

2. What Happens During Lookup
When you call
dict.ContainsKey(p2)
Dictionary does:
	1.	Compute p2.GetHashCode() -> this is generic function
	2.	Go to that bucket
	3.	Compare keys in that bucket using Equals()
So
Step 1: hash match → bucket
Step 2: Equals() → actual key match

3. Role of GetHashCode() vs Equals()

GetHashCode()
	•	Determines which bucket to search
	•	Must be same for equal objects

Equals()
	•	Determines if two keys are actually equal

Interview line:

HashCode narrows the search to a bucket, Equals confirms the exact key.

4. Default Behavior (No Override)

If you don’t override anything:
	•	GetHashCode() → based on object identity
	•	Equals() → reference equality
Meaning
Person p1 = new Person();
Person p2 = new Person();
Even if data is same
p1 != p2
because they are different objects in heap.

5. Your Special Case
You wrote
public override bool Equals(object obj)
{
    return true;
}

Meaning:

Every object is equal to every other object.

But since:
GetHashCode() = base.GetHashCode()

each object gets a different bucket, so the dictionary never even compares Equals.

Result:
dict.ContainsKey(p2) → false
because it searches a different bucket.

6. What “Dictionary Breaks” Means

Example:
Person p = new Person { Name="A" };
dict[p] = "First";

p.Name = "B";
If GetHashCode() uses Name, then:
Old hash = based on "A"
New hash = based on "B"

Dictionary stored the object in the old bucket, but lookup now goes to the new bucket.

Result:
dict.ContainsKey(p) → false
Even though the object exists inside dictionary.

This is what we mean by:

The dictionary becomes inconsistent or “breaks”.

7. Important Rule (Interview Gold)

Keys in a dictionary must be immutable.

Example of good keys:
	•	string
	•	int
	•	immutable objects

Bad keys:
class Person
{
   public string Name;  // mutable
}
Better
class Person
{
   public readonly string Name;
}

Dictionary in C# is implemented using a hash table. When a key is added, its GetHashCode() determines the bucket where it is stored. 
During lookup, the dictionary computes the hash again to locate the bucket and then uses Equals() to find the exact key.
If GetHashCode() and Equals() are inconsistent, lookups can fail. 
Also, dictionary keys should be immutable because changing a key’s state can change its hash and make the entry unreachable.



using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
					
public class Program
{
	public static void Main()
	{
		Dictionary<string, int> dict = new Dictionary<string, int>();
		dict.Add("apple",19);
		dict.Add("oranges",20);
		
		foreach(var item in dict)
		{
			Console.WriteLine($"Value is {item.Value}");
			Console.WriteLine($"Value is {item.Key}");
			Console.WriteLine($"Value is {item.Key.GetHashCode()}");
		}
		Console.WriteLine("Hello World");
		Person p1 = new Person();
		Person p2 = new Person();
		p1.Name = "A";
		p2.Name = "B";
		Dictionary<Person, string> dict2 = new Dictionary<Person, string>();
		dict2[p1] = "First";
		Console.WriteLine("Display");
		Console.WriteLine(dict2[p1]);
		p1.Name = "B";
		dict2.Remove(p1);
		Console.WriteLine(dict2[p1]);
		Console.WriteLine(dict2.ContainsKey(p2));
		
	}
}

class Person
{
	public string Name;
	public override bool Equals(object obj)
	{
		Person p = obj as Person;
		return Name == p.Name;
//		return true;
	}
	
	public override int GetHashCode()
	{
		return Name.GetHashCode();
		//return base.GetHashCode();
	}
}
